  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There is an N×MN\\times MN×M grid.\n\nThe rows are numbered 111 to NNN from top to bottom and columns are numbered 111 to MMM from left to right. Alice is at coordinate (X,Y)(X,Y)(X,Y).\n\nAlice needs to calculate the bitwise XOR of Manhattan distances of all the coordinates in the grid from the point (X,Y)(X,Y)(X,Y).\n\nNote that Manhattan distance between points (X1,Y1)(X_1,Y_1)(X1​,Y1​) and (X2,Y2)(X_2,Y_2)(X2​,Y2​) is calculated as ∣X1−X2∣+∣Y1−Y2∣|X_1 - X_2| + |Y_1 - Y_2|∣X1​−X2​∣+∣Y1​−Y2​∣.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and MMM — the number of rows and the number of columns in the grid, respectively.\nThe next line contains two space-separated integers XXX and YYY - Alice's Coordinates.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the bitwise XOR of Manhattan distances of all the coordinates in the grid from the point (X,Y)(X,Y)(X,Y).",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1051 \\leq T \\leq 10^51≤T≤105",
      "1≤N,M≤1091 \\leq N, M \\leq 10^91≤N,M≤109",
      "1≤X≤N1 \\leq X \\leq N1≤X≤N",
      "1≤Y≤M1 \\leq Y \\leq M1≤Y≤M"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n3 3 2 2\n2 7 1 4\n1 1 1 1\n4 4 2 2",
        "output": "0\n1\n0\n4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Alice is at coordinate (2,2).\n\nThe Manhattan distance between (2,2)(2,2)(2,2) and (2,2)(2,2)(2,2) is 000.\nThe Manhattan distance between {(1,2),(2,1),(3,2),(2,3)}\\{(1,2) , (2,1) , (3,2) , (2,3)\\}{(1,2),(2,1),(3,2),(2,3)} and (2,2)(2,2)(2,2) is 111.\nThe Manhattan distance between {(1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(3,3)}\\{(1,1) , (1,3) , (3,1) , (3,3)\\}{(1,1),(1,3),(3,1),(3,3)} and (2,2)(2,2)(2,2) is 222.\n\nSo, the bitwise XOR of Manhattan distances from point (2,2)(2,2)(2,2) is 000."
      }
    ]
  },
